V(WPW, IO_SV,
  "A 20-year-old man with a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 220 converts to sinus rhythm with adenosine. An accessory pathway is suspected. Which tracing is most useful for looking for it?",
  [["A tracing in sinus rhythm",
    "Correct. Pre-excitation, a short PR interval and a slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave), on a tracing in normal sinus rhythm is consistent with an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) and the potential for atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia. His narrow tachycardia at 220 that converted with adenosine fits the orthodromic form, so the pathway is sought in sinus rhythm."],
   ["A tracing during the tachycardia",
    "A tracing during the tachycardia is not where the pathway is sought. Orthodromic reentry, the more common form, uses antegrade conduction through the atrioventricular node and gives a regular, narrow complex rhythm with a retrograde P wave after the QRS. Pre-excitation, a short PR and delta wave, is documented on tracings in sinus rhythm. Tell: look for the pathway on the sinus-rhythm tracing.", "reversed direction or value"],
   ["A serum electrolyte panel",
    "A serum electrolyte panel does not reveal an accessory pathway; electrolyte abnormalities appear instead among causes of sick sinus syndrome. The pathway is found by pre-excitation, a short PR with a delta wave, on a tracing in sinus rhythm. Tell: an accessory pathway is an electrical finding on the tracing, not a laboratory one.", "adjacent fact"],
   ["A coronary angiogram",
    "A coronary angiogram images the coronary arteries and cannot show an accessory pathway, which is an electrical finding. That finding, a short PR and delta wave, appears on a tracing in sinus rhythm. Tell: look for pre-excitation on the sinus-rhythm tracing.", "confused mechanism or drug class"]],
  "%s, Slides 33, 34" % DECK1, "initial test", "test"),

